{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Wrestlings of Elohim.</b> Menachem ben Saruk explained it in the construct form צָמִיד פָּתִיל — joinings from the Omnipresent: I was joined with my sister to merit children. And I explain it as a term עִקֵּשׁ וּפְתַלְתֹּל (Deuteronomy 32) — I twisted and pressed with many pressings and wrestlings to the Omnipresent, to be equal to my sister." "<b>And I prevailed.</b> He agreed through me. And Onkelos rendered it in the language of prayer — like 'wrestlings of Elohim: I prayed with beloved petitions before Him; I was received, and I was answered like my sister.'" "<b>I wrestled.</b> My prayer was accepted. And from an alef there are many in the language of notarikon."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {2 "Rashi's point is that the נפתולי/נפתלתי root can be read notarikon-style as נפתל-תי, where the final alef (מ״א) is one of several possible notarikon readings."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:01:13.872293934Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}